import{_ as o,o as t,c as s,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Decisions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"decisions.md","filePath":"decisions.md","lastUpdated":1793726242000}'),r={name:"decisions.md"};function n(d,e,i,c,l,h){return t(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="decisions" tabindex="-1">Decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decisions&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The architecture decisions behind kernl, one entry per <code>D-NNN</code>. Every <code>D-NNN</code> mentioned in these docs links to its entry here, at the anchor <code>#d-nnn</code>. The log is <code>DECISIONS.md</code> at the root of the source repository, which is private during the alpha; this page includes that file verbatim when the docs are built, so it is never a second copy that can drift.</p><p>Architecture decisions for kernl, newest last. Each entry records what was decided, why, and what it would take to reverse it. A decision stays here after it is reversed; it gets a <code>Superseded by</code> line instead of being deleted.</p><p>Format: <code>D-NNN · title</code> → Context, Decision, Consequences, Reversal cost.</p><hr><h2 id="d-001" tabindex="-1">D-001 · kernl composes kehto; it does not fork it <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-001" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-001 · kernl composes kehto; it does not fork it&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> kehto publishes <code>@kehto/{acl,firewall,runtime,shell,services,nip,wm}</code> as the unopinionated NIP-5D toolkit. kernl is the opinionated shell. Two of kehto&#39;s seams are narrower than kernl wants: <code>ShellAdapter</code> does not forward <code>getFocusContext</code>, <code>onFirewallEvent</code> or <code>firewallPersistence</code> to the runtime, and <code>createRuntime</code> hardcodes the ACL default policy to <code>&#39;permissive&#39;</code>. The transport internals (<code>createNip5dTransport</code>, <code>createShellReadyState</code>) are not exported, so kernl cannot assemble its own bridge from kehto parts.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> kernl depends on the published <code>@kehto/*</code> packages at pinned minor ranges and uses <code>createShellBridge</code> unchanged. Behaviour kehto does not expose is built in front of the bridge, not inside a fork:</p><ul><li>Focus-aware rate limiting runs in <code>@kernl/guard</code> using the pure <code>@kehto/firewall</code> <code>evaluate()</code> engine with focus supplied by kernl&#39;s window manager. The runtime&#39;s built-in firewall is additionally tightened through <code>bridge.runtime.firewallState</code>.</li><li>A restrictive grant posture is produced with <code>capabilities.resolveEnvironment</code>, which narrows the advertised domains per applet identity until the user grants them, plus explicit <code>aclState.grant</code> calls. The runtime&#39;s permissive default is never reached for a domain the user has not granted.</li></ul><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Four upstream gaps are recorded against kehto/web rather than patched locally: (1) forward the firewall hooks through <code>ShellAdapter</code>; (2) let the host choose the ACL default policy; (3) offer a pre-dispatch hook on port traffic, since <code>onNappletMessage</code> is observe-only; (4) expose a setter for the runtime firewall&#39;s <code>unfocusedMultiplier</code>. When any of them lands, the corresponding kernl layer shrinks (see D-012).</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low. Both local layers sit behind interfaces in <code>@kernl/guard</code> and <code>@kernl/core</code> and can be deleted when upstream provides the hook.</p><h2 id="d-002" tabindex="-1">D-002 · applesauce v7 is the only event layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-002" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-002 · applesauce v7 is the only event layer&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> kehto&#39;s reference runtime (Paja) uses <code>nostr-tools</code> <code>SimplePool</code>. kernl&#39;s first opinion is that applesauce is the event layer.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>applesauce-core</code> (<code>EventStore</code>), <code>applesauce-relay</code> (<code>RelayPool</code>), <code>applesauce-signers</code>, <code>applesauce-accounts</code> (<code>AccountManager</code>), <code>applesauce-loaders</code> and <code>applesauce-factory</code> are the only libraries kernl source imports for events, relays, signing and accounts. <code>nostr-tools</code> stays a transitive peer dependency required by kehto and applesauce; kernl source does not import it. kehto&#39;s <code>RelayPoolLike</code>, <code>RelayPoolServiceOptions</code> and <code>DmRelayPool</code> contracts are satisfied by thin adapters over one shared <code>RelayPool</code> in <code>@kernl/core/applesauce</code>.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> One pool, one store, one account manager per shell. A guard test fails the build if <code>nostr-tools</code> is imported from kernl source.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> High by design. The adapters are small, but the opinion is the point.</p><h2 id="d-003" tabindex="-1">D-003 · Every kehto mitigation ships on, in its strict profile <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-003" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-003 · Every kehto mitigation ships on, in its strict profile&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> Opinion three in <code>SOUL.md</code>.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>@kernl/guard</code> exposes three profiles, <code>strict</code>, <code>balanced</code> and <code>relaxed</code>. The shell boots in <code>strict</code>. The profile can be loosened per applet by the user through the grants panel, never globally by configuration. The mitigations and their defaults are listed in <code>docs/concepts/guard.md</code> and surfaced in the UI as a readable list with reasons.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Some applets will hit limits in <code>strict</code>. The guard emits an event for every rejection and deferral so the user can see what happened and decide.</p><p><strong>Status in the first draft.</strong> The profile is shell-wide and not persisted: <code>GuardFacade.setProfile</code> rebuilds the single pipeline, and windows already open keep the runtime rates installed at mount. Per-applet, remembered loosening is the target this decision describes and is tracked in <code>docs/roadmap.md</code>. The default remains <code>strict</code> on every boot, so the gap errs toward the user.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> A one-line profile change for a user; a <code>SOUL.md</code> amendment for the project.</p><h2 id="d-004" tabindex="-1">D-004 · Four packages, one app, one docs site <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-004" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-004 · Four packages, one app, one docs site&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> The repository needs a shape that lets docs, tests and publishing stay per-package without forcing the shell UI into a framework-free constraint.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Workspace</th><th>Name</th><th>Role</th><th>Framework</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>packages/guard</code></td><td><code>@kernl/guard</code></td><td>Ingress flood control, visibility gate, focus-aware firewall, CSP profile, mitigation descriptions. Pure.</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>packages/messaging</code></td><td><code>@kernl/messaging</code></td><td>Messaging provider contract, registry, composite NAP-DM adapter, provider descriptions. Pure.</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>packages/bridges</code></td><td><code>@kernl/bridges</code></td><td>Locator parsing, bridge contract, registry, resource resolver with SSRF policy. Pure.</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>packages/core</code></td><td><code>@kernl/core</code></td><td>Composition root: applesauce adapters, applet resolution and mount plan, consent summaries, layouts, persistence. Browser-agnostic.</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>apps/web</code></td><td><code>@kernl/web</code></td><td>Landing site at <code>/</code> and the shell at <code>/app/</code>.</td><td>Astro 7, Svelte 5 islands</td></tr><tr><td><code>docs</code></td><td><code>@kernl/docs</code></td><td>Documentation at <code>/docs/</code>.</td><td>VitePress</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Published packages have zero framework dependencies, matching kehto&#39;s rule. The app is free to use Svelte.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> TypeDoc runs over the four packages. Each has a <code>docs/packages/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> page with a version row, enforced by <code>scripts/audit-docs.mjs</code>.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Medium. Merging packages is cheap; splitting the app later is not.</p><h2 id="d-005" tabindex="-1">D-005 · Behaviour is specified in Gherkin and executed by vitest <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-005" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-005 · Behaviour is specified in Gherkin and executed by vitest&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> The requirement is BDD with full coverage. kehto and napplet both use plain vitest.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> Every package has a <code>features/</code> directory of <code>.feature</code> files run by <a href="https://github.com/dnotes/quickpickle" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">quickpickle</a> inside vitest, with step definitions in <code>features/steps/</code>. Coverage thresholds are 100% for statements, branches, functions and lines over <code>packages/*/src</code>. Plain <code>*.test.ts</code> files are allowed only for property-style checks that do not read as behaviour; they do not substitute for a feature.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> A behaviour without a scenario is not merged. Coverage is enforced in CI through <code>vitest run --coverage</code>.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low mechanically, high culturally.</p><h2 id="d-006" tabindex="-1">D-006 · The public site never names a protocol <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-006" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-006 · The public site never names a protocol&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> Requirement five: the website is gorgeous and does not say the protocol&#39;s name.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>apps/web</code> output is scanned by <code>scripts/check-public-vocabulary.mjs</code> for a denylist (<code>nostr</code>, <code>nip-</code>, <code>napplet</code>, <code>relay</code>, <code>pubkey</code>, <code>npub</code>, <code>nsec</code>) and the build fails on a hit. The denylist applies to the landing pages and the shell UI copy. It does not apply to <code>docs/</code>, which is written for developers and must name things precisely.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> UI copy says &quot;applet&quot;, &quot;account&quot;, &quot;network&quot;, &quot;provider&quot;, &quot;guard&quot;. Developer-facing terms live in the docs.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low.</p><h2 id="d-007" tabindex="-1">D-007 · The site is agent-readable by construction <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-007" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-007 · The site is agent-readable by construction&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> Requirement: an agent-readiness score of 84 or higher, <code>llms.txt</code>, WebMCP where sensible.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The Astro build emits <code>/llms.txt</code>, <code>/llms-full.txt</code>, a Markdown twin for every page with <code>&lt;link rel=&quot;alternate&quot; type=&quot;text/markdown&quot;&gt;</code>, <code>/robots.txt</code> that allows the common AI crawlers and references the sitemap, JSON-LD (<code>WebApplication</code>, <code>SoftwareSourceCode</code>), Open Graph and canonical metadata, and semantic forms with WebMCP declarative attributes plus a guarded <code>navigator.modelContext.registerTool</code> registration. The docs build emits <code>/docs/llms.txt</code> and <code>/docs/llms-full.txt</code>. <code>scripts/audit-agent-readiness.mjs</code> checks the built artifact for every item.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Agent discovery is part of the build contract, not a later task.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low.</p><h2 id="d-008" tabindex="-1">D-008 · Repository and deployment <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-008" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-008 · Repository and deployment&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> The GitHub organisation is <code>kernlize</code>; the repository is <code>kernlize/web</code>. No custom domain is decided.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> URLs in package metadata point at <code>https://github.com/kernlize/web</code>. The Pages artifact is built with a configurable <code>SITE_BASE</code> (default <code>/</code>) and <code>SITE_URL</code>; the Pages workflow reads both from repository variables and falls back to <code>/web/</code> and <code>https://kernlize.github.io/web/</code>. When a domain is chosen, only the variables change. See D-013 for why production should be a domain root.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Absolute URLs in <code>llms.txt</code>, sitemap, canonical tags and JSON-LD are derived from <code>SITE_URL</code> at build time.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low.</p><h2 id="d-009" tabindex="-1">D-009 · Messaging providers describe themselves; the shell does not rank them <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-009" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-009 · Messaging providers describe themselves; the shell does not rank them&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> Opinion five.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>MessagingProvider.properties</code> is a fixed, truthful set of fields (forward secrecy, post-compromise security, metadata protection, same-client requirement, group support, specification reference). The shell renders these fields verbatim in the provider picker. There is no &quot;recommended&quot; flag. The first draft ships three providers: <code>private</code> (gift-wrapped private messages), <code>legacy</code> (the original direct-message format, marked as metadata-exposing), and <code>ratchet</code> (double-ratchet sessions, marked experimental and available only when its runtime is supplied).</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Adding a provider is a package change plus a docs page. Removing the ranking is not possible because there is none.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low.</p><h2 id="d-010" tabindex="-1">D-010 · Bridges are named by what they do <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-010" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-010 · Bridges are named by what they do&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> Opinion four.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>@kernl/bridges</code> defines a <code>Bridge</code> with a user-facing <code>label</code> (<code>downloads</code>, <code>mail</code>) and a list of locator schemes (<code>magnet:</code>, <code>nzb:</code>, <code>slsk:</code>, <code>mailto:</code>). The first draft ships a complete HTTP bridge with SSRF policy and four parsers with backend interfaces (<code>TorrentBackend</code>, <code>NzbBackend</code>, <code>SoulseekBackend</code>, <code>MailBackend</code>). Without a backend, a bridge reports <code>unavailable</code> and the shell shows that honestly. Backends are out of scope for this draft and are tracked in <code>docs/roadmap.md</code>.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> The bridge contract is exercised end to end by the HTTP bridge, so later backends plug into a tested path.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low.</p><h2 id="d-011" tabindex="-1">D-011 · Preview mode is a product feature, not a test double <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-011" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-011 · Preview mode is a product feature, not a test double&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> The shell needs to be usable without an account, a network or a published applet: for people evaluating it, for the Playwright suite, and for the landing page&#39;s &quot;open&quot; form when nothing real is reachable.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> <code>apps/web/src/shell/preview-kernl.ts</code> implements the same <code>Kernl</code> contract as <code>@kernl/core</code> using the real <code>@kernl/guard</code>, <code>@kernl/messaging</code> (memory backend with a scripted correspondent) and <code>@kernl/bridges</code> packages, a real applesauce <code>AccountManager</code>, and two sample applets whose <code>srcdoc</code> carries the guard&#39;s CSP and exercises consent, notifications, flooding and link opening. <code>?preview=1</code> selects it; the live path falls back to it only when <code>createKernl</code> is unavailable, and says so in a notice.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Preview behaviour is held to the same vocabulary and accessibility rules as the live shell. Sample applets are the first place a new mitigation is demonstrated.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low; the preview is one module behind the contract.</p><h2 id="d-012" tabindex="-1">D-012 · Guard enforcement is layered: configured before dispatch, reactive after <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-012" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-012 · Guard enforcement is layered: configured before dispatch, reactive after&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> kehto&#39;s bridge owns the <code>MessagePort</code> an applet speaks over, and exposes applet traffic to the host only through the observe-only <code>onNappletMessage</code> hook. kernl cannot sit between the port and the runtime (D-001).</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> Two layers. Before dispatch, kernl configures kehto&#39;s own runtime firewall from the guard profile when an applet is mounted (<code>applyRuntimeFirewallRules</code>, translating op classes through <code>RUNTIME_OP_CLASSES</code>), so kehto rejects over-budget envelopes itself. After dispatch, kernl&#39;s ingress guard, visibility gate and focus firewall observe the same traffic with the shell&#39;s focus and visibility; a <code>quarantine</code> disposition revokes the applet&#39;s binding, and every other non-pass disposition is surfaced to the user as a guard event. The window-level <code>hello</code> is the one message kernl filters before kehto sees it.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Deferral while hidden is advisory in this draft: the applet&#39;s envelope has already reached the runtime by the time the gate observes it, so the gate&#39;s effect is the tightened budget and the event trail, not a held queue. The upstream request that would make it a true pre-dispatch hook is recorded with D-001&#39;s gaps.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low; the pipeline sits behind <code>createGuardPipeline</code> and shrinks when kehto exposes a pre-dispatch hook.</p><h2 id="d-013" tabindex="-1">D-013 · The site is served at a domain root <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-013" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D-013 · The site is served at a domain root&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Context.</strong> D-007 sets an agent-readiness score of 84 or higher. Measured with amiagentready on 2026-11-03, the scanner works on the <strong>origin</strong>: it scores the domain&#39;s homepage whatever path it is given, and reads <code>robots.txt</code> and <code>llms.txt</code> from the domain root. The scoring is reproducible from its response: each scored check contributes its weight, pass at full, warn at half, fail at zero.</p><p><strong>Measurement.</strong> The Pages artifact served at the root of a temporary public origin, through <code>scripts/serve-pages.mjs</code>, scored <strong>92</strong> (12 pass, 2 warn, 0 fail; WebMCP detected). The two warns are &quot;online booking&quot; (kernl has none) and &quot;schema coverage&quot; (asks for local-business types such as opening hours and ratings, which do not apply). The same origin hosts <code>/docs/</code>, so the one score covers the website and the docs.</p><p><strong>Decision.</strong> Production serves the Pages artifact at the root of its own domain, and the host answers <code>Accept: text/markdown</code> with the page&#39;s Markdown twin (<code>Vary: Accept</code>), as <code>scripts/serve-pages.mjs</code> does. The Pages workflow reads <code>SITE_URL</code> and <code>SITE_BASE</code> from repository variables, so a custom domain needs no code change.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Served from the project subpath <code>kernlize.github.io/web/</code> (D-008&#39;s default), the score drops to about 82 because the origin has no <code>robots.txt</code> or <code>llms.txt</code>. A static host without content negotiation scores 90 at a root. Both regressions are visible in <code>docs/how-tos/deploy.md</code>.</p><p><strong>Production, 2026-11-03.</strong> The artifact is published to the public <code>kernlize/kernlize.github.io</code> repository (built output only; this repository stays private) and served at <a href="https://kernlize.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://kernlize.github.io/</a>. Live amiagentready score: <strong>90</strong>, no failing checks; the site and <code>/docs/</code> share the score because the tool grades the origin. The browser suite, including the public-vocabulary walk-through, passes against the live URL (<code>PLAYWRIGHT_BASE_URL=https://kernlize.github.io</code>).</p><p><strong>Publishing is manual, by the maintainer&#39;s choice (2026-11-03).</strong> <code>pnpm site:publish</code> builds, runs the vocabulary and agent-readiness gates, and pushes <code>.pages/</code> to the site repository. CI builds and audits on every push; it would publish only if a scoped <code>SITE_PUBLISH_TOKEN</code> were added, and none is.</p><p><strong>Reversal cost.</strong> Low; only deployment configuration.</p>',77)])])}const g=o(r,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
